# No source-inspection tests (HARD GUARDRAIL)

Tests must **import and run** production TypeScript and assert behaviour. Never treat production `.ts` / `.js` as a string to grep, `includes`, or regex.

**Do not add this to CI.** Do not add a GitHub Actions step or `assert-no-source-inspection` npm script. Agents must simply never write those tests.

## Forbidden

| NEVER | Why |
|-------|-----|
| `readFileSync` / `fs.readFile` of production `.ts` or `.js` as the assertion | Source text is not a contract; refactors fail tests without changing behaviour |
| Scanning `src/**/*.ts` for string tokens (`export`, route paths, handler names) | Same — implementation shape, not behaviour |
| `?raw` imports of production TypeScript/JavaScript in specs | Same |

## Allowed

| OK | Notes |
|----|--------|
| Import production modules and call them / hit handlers | The test **runs** the code |
| Sass / HTML fixtures (`readFileSync` of `.sass`, `.scss`, `.html`) | Layout/style contracts |
| OpenAPI JSON / JSON / JSONC / markdown / wrangler as **data** | Not production TS |
| Copied fixture files that are themselves test data (e.g. `submit-url-contract.ts` imported as a module) | Import and use; do not `readFileSync` production `submit.ts` |

## If unsure

Write a behavioural test. Do not open production source as text.
